  "live_records": {
    "q-2343": {
      "dom_id": "step-q2343",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for \n $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}$ on the interval $[0,4].$",
      "worked_solution": "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,4]$ and differentiable on $(0,4),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (0,4)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(4)-f(0)}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{4}-2\\sqrt{0}}{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{c}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{c}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{c} & =1 \\\\ c & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
