    "q-77862": {
      "dom_id": "step-q77862",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[2,4]?$\n\nI. $f(x)={e}^{x}+1$\n\nII. $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n\nIII. $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$",
      "worked_solution": "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[2,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[2,4]$ and differentiable on $(2,4).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{x}+1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[2,4]$ and differentiable on $(2,4).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[2,4]$ and differentiable on $(2,4).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[2,4]$ and differentiable on $(2,4).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, the functions I, II, and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[2,4].$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
