    "q-77866": {
      "dom_id": "step-q77866",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1]?$\n\nI. $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}$\n\nII. $g(x)={x}^{2}-1$\n\nIII. $h(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}-1}$",
      "worked_solution": "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1],$ the function must be continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-1}$ is not defined at $x=\\pm 1,$ hence, it is not continuous at $x=\\pm 1,$ and so it is not continuous on $[-1,1].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{2}-1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}-1}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-1,1]$ and differentiable on $(-1,1).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions II and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-1,1].$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
